WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.058
RaceLab is new hackathon work by Tang Vu. It reuses
Meta transport and

00:00:06.058 --> 00:00:12.117
local infrastructure from the earlier event
prototype, LiveWorld. TEND is an existing MIT

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:18.175
source input. Here is a familiar failure: a worker
saves its claim, then

00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:22.836
stops. The job exists, but another worker cannot
finish it.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.761
RaceLab verifies the exact public source revision,
extracts this TypeScript claim method, and

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:35.523
executes it through a bounded SQLite adapter. Without
interruption, one due job is

00:00:35.523 --> 00:00:41.284
claimed and completed. The original method passes.
These process identifiers and transaction receipts

00:00:41.284 --> 00:00:43.500
come from the local execution.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.887
Now stop worker A after its claim commits. RaceLab
observes its exit and

00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:55.775
starts worker B on the same database file. The
original query only selects

00:00:55.775 --> 00:01:01.662
scheduled or retrying jobs. This row is already
claimed, so it stays behind.

00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:06.191
The completion count is zero. The independent
recovery check fails.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.940
Muse Spark inspected this method and the declared
recovery contract. In the retained

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:18.879
investigation, it ran a failing crash probe and
proposed one replacement method. Check

00:01:18.879 --> 00:01:24.819
Meta repair executes that recorded proposal again
locally, with no new model call.

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:30.759
Worker B now reclaims the expired job. One local
completion is recorded. Original

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:33.500
A and candidate B remain inspectable.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.875
Recovering one job is not enough. A blanket reset
could steal a healthy

00:01:39.875 --> 00:01:45.750
worker's claim. The fixed checker also requires
preservation of fresh claims, future jobs,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:51.625
completed work, and payloads. It checks retries,
mixed rows, the exact sixty second

00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:57.500
lease boundary, and a changed input. This candidate
passes all ten declared conditions.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.274
Inspect the expired owner condition. Worker B
receives a newer durable generation. When

00:02:03.274 --> 00:02:08.547
worker A tries to complete with the old generation,
the fixed local guard

00:02:08.547 --> 00:02:13.821
rejects it. Scrub the transaction trace to inspect
what committed. This guard belongs

00:02:13.821 --> 00:02:19.094
to RaceLab's fixture. It does not establish exactly
once delivery to an external

00:02:19.094 --> 00:02:19.500
service.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.947
Export the regression and reopen that file. RaceLab
discards supplied verdicts, traces, and

00:02:25.947 --> 00:02:31.894
process identifiers, then executes the source and
conditions again. Imported model attribution is

00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:37.840
unverified. A method diff targets the pinned source.
Editing clears old results, so

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.500
a previous pass cannot silently survive a change.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.170
This is one repository derived study, with a new
lease contract, a bounded

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:52.341
adapter, real SQLite, and controlled process
sequences. It does not prove whole application

00:02:52.341 --> 00:02:57.511
correctness or general repair performance. RaceLab
turns a model's proposed repair into evidence

00:02:57.511 --> 00:02:59.500
you can replay and challenge.
